#version 450
//! Append one position to a KV cache, at the row the step supplies.
//!
//! # Why an op rather than a copy
//!
//! `Op::Copy` is a `vkCmdCopyBuffer` with its offsets baked into the recording, and the row a
//! decode step writes moves by one every token. Recording it is therefore exactly the thing that
//! forced a re-record per token. This is the same copy with the destination row read from
//! `step_params.prefix` instead, which is the whole difference between a plan that is recorded
//! once and one that is recorded per token.
//!
//! # Why the projection does not just write here directly
//!
//! It could, in principle: the K and V projections produce this row, so a convolution that knew
//! about the cache could store into it. That would put a dynamic destination into
//! `conv_point_int8.comp` and every other projection shader, for one caller. Keeping it a
//! separate op costs one `d_model` copy per layer per step - 1024 fp16 elements for NLLB, against
//! the 1024x1024 matrix multiply that produced them - and leaves every projection shader alone.
//!
//! # Push fields
//!
//! `p.in0` is the source row, `d_model` contiguous elements. `p.out_at` is the base of the cache,
//! which is `[max_positions, 1, d_model]`. `p.in_c` is `d_model`, the distance between rows.
//! `p.in_h` is `max_positions`, read only to refuse a write past the end. `p.count` is `d_model`.
//!
//! `out_c`/`out_h`/`out_w` are left as the cache's true dimensions rather than repurposed for the
//! bound, because their product is what `assert_no_aliasing` treats as the region this op writes.

#include "common.glsl"

void main() {
    uint index = global_index();
    if (index >= p.count) {
        return;
    }
    // A cache that has run out of room drops the position rather than corrupting whatever the
    // arena put after it. The caller is expected to stop before this; a shader cannot report it,
    // so the safe failure is the silent one rather than an out-of-bounds store.
    // `p.group` is the positions being written: 0 or 1 is the decode step's single row, more is
    // a prefill's whole prompt.
    //
    // The two need different addressing, and this is the one place the layouts disagree. A cache
    // is position-major - position `t`, channel `c` at `t * width + c`. A prefill's K and V are
    // `[width, 1, T]`, so they are **channel**-major: `c * T + t`. For one position the two
    // coincide and a reshape is free; for more it is a transpose, and doing it here costs one
    // divide rather than a whole extra op and a second pass over the arena.
    uint positions = max(p.group, 1u);
    uint channel = index / positions;
    uint position = index % positions;
    if (step_params.prefix + position >= p.in_h) {
        return;
    }
    arena[p.out_at + (step_params.prefix + position) * p.in_c + channel] = arena[p.in0 + index];
}
